+----------------------+
|                      |
|  1. What's new?      |
|                      |
+----------------------+

This is Python version 2.7.18 for AmigaOS - the final Python 2 release,
ported from Irmen de Jong's classic AmigaPython 2.0 line.

See Docs/NEWS for a summary of language and library changes since Python 2.0,
and Docs/Amiga for Amiga-specific modules and behaviour. Full upstream
"What's New" documents for each 2.x release are at https://docs.python.org/.

Notable Amiga changes since the 2.0 port:

* Interpreter and library are based on Python 2.7.18 (not 2.0).
* The builtin `amiga` module gained many POSIX-style APIs (access, sleep,
  waitpid, pathconf/sysconf, urandom, fsync, closerange, and more).
* Former Doslib entry points, ArgParser helpers, and path converters
  (`to_unix` / `from_unix`) are available on the `amiga` module.
* ASL FileRequest / MessageBox, locale OpenCatalog, and icon DiskObject
  helpers are registered on `amiga` (OS4-style parity where practical).
* Networking targets modern bsdsocket.library (e.g. Roadshow); I-Net225
  remains unsupported (as since late 1.5.x).
* Build is aimed at ToolKit-compatible Amiga toolchains (SAS/C and/or
  VBCC/PosixLib depending on the Source tree you use).


+----------------------+
|                      |
|  2. General remarks  |
|                      |
+----------------------+

* The MAIN PYTHON SITE is <https://www.python.org>.  Tutorial, language
  reference, and library reference for Python 2.7 are at
  <https://docs.python.org/2.7/>.

* AmigaPython pages (amigazen project):
	https://github.com/amigazen/amigapython/
	http://www.amigazen.com/amigapython/

* In the `Docs/Amiga' directory you can find the docs for the Amiga specific
  modules and features. Read them, they contain vital information!

* The Python library contains an extensive test set.
  To try it yourself, type (after installation):
	Python -c "import test.autotest"
  (You can also start the RunTest.py script from the Workbench).
  Some tests cannot run on the Amiga - they will be skipped.
  If you don't start a TCP stack first, test_socket and test_select will fail.
  If usergroup.library cannot be opened, crypt, grp and pwd will fail.

* To see some command line options, use the -? option, or any other
  unrecognised option.

* To run a speed benchmark, type:
	Python -c "import test.pystone; test.pystone.main()"

* In the `Icons' directory there are some icon suggestions.
  def_py.info is an icon for Python source files (.py)
  def_pyc.info is an icon for Python bytecode files (.pyc)

* Amiga-specific questions: toolkit@amigazen.com or the project GitHub.
  General Python questions: https://www.python.org/ (and historic
  newsgroup/archives for Python 2).


+-----------------------+
|                       |
|  3. Troubleshooting   |
|                       |
+-----------------------+

* When I try to install Python, I get "Unable to open your tool
  'installer'"!

  The Installer utility is required for installation, but it is not included
  in the archive.  Get it from somewhere else and copy it to your C:
  directory or somewhere where Workbench can find it.

* When I start python, I get "This program requires a math co-processor"!

  You need at least a 68020/68030 class CPU; an FPU is required for builds
  that expect a floating-point unit.

* I get "Couldn't open bsdsocket.library" or "Couldn't open
  usergroup.library" errors!

  You are trying to use functions that need one of these libraries.  A TCP
  stack that provides bsdsocket.library is required for network modules.
  usergroup.library is needed for pwd/grp/crypt-style APIs.

* Python crashes when executing complex (recursive) code!

  This should not happen; you should get a MemoryError exception.  If it does
  crash, increase your stack size and try again.  Default stack needs are
  higher than in the old 2.0 binary; raise the stack if you see crashes on
  deep recursion or large imports.

* Functions which use the PIPE: device (like os.popen) seem to work
  incorrectly!

  The default l:queue-handler is buggy.  Make sure you have installed a fixed
  l:queue-handler (e.g. util/sys/HWGQueue.lha from Aminet).

* Other errors

  Report Amiga-specific problems via amigazen project.


